// src/net_api.pdx -- libpdx-net.M1-002 (libpdx-net#2)
//
// Public API surface stubs for the R100 client-side networking SDK.
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE (design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md §2.1 + §13.1)
// =============================================================================
//
// Publishes the nine public entry-point symbols the R100 API
// contract advertises to any consumer that statically links
// libpdx-net. At v0.2.0 every entry-point was a fail-closed stub:
// each function returned a `*_ERR_NOT_IMPL` sentinel from its
// corresponding NetTypes.pdx band (NET/DNS/TLS) so a caller could
// discriminate "the SDK is a scaffold" from "the network refused
// the operation" without inspecting a second slot.
//
// The nine symbols land here so tools that statically link
// libpdx-net (pdxcurl, pdxdig, pdxsock, ...) get a stable ld
// resolution TODAY, and every future real-body landing is an
// in-place body swap at the same symbol -- callers do not
// re-link when M2-001..M2-007 or M3-001..M3-004 flip from stub
// to real.
//
// M2-001 (#4) is the first of those swaps: `net_socket` /
// `net_connect` / `net_send` / `net_recv` / `net_close` are now
// real, one-hop delegations to net_tcp.pdx's `net_tcp_*` raw
// syscall trampolines (SC+ 87/91/92/93/3) rather than
// `mov rax,<NOT_IMPL>; ret` stubs -- see the "SWAP DISCIPLINE"
// section below and net_tcp.pdx's own header for why the real
// bodies live in a separate symbol rather than inline here.
// `net_inet_pton` swaps too (M2-004, #7): it becomes a thin
// adapter over net_inet.pdx's real `net_inet_pton_raw` engine
// (module-suffixed there to dodge the same flat-global-symbol
// collision net_tcp.pdx's `net_tcp_*` naming dodges), folding that
// engine's out-pointer result back into this stub's original
// two-argument, direct-return public contract. `net_resolve` and
// `net_tls_wrap` remain stubs.
//
// M2-002 (#5) swaps `net_bind` too: it becomes a thin adapter over
// net_server.pdx's real `net_server_bind(fd, local_port)` engine
// (the live kernel ABI has no sockaddr concept at all -- see
// net_server.pdx's own header for the trust-the-live-code
// confirmation). This file's original `net_bind(sock, sa_va, sa_len)`
// public contract is preserved; `sa_va` is read as a pointer to a
// single 8-byte `local_port` value (the smallest sockaddr-shaped
// generalization that still matches the kernel's actual (fd, port)
// ABI) and `sa_len` is bound-checked >= 8. This same landing ALSO
// adds two brand-new public entries, `net_listen(sock, backlog)` and
// `net_accept(sock)`, as plain one-hop delegations to net_server.pdx's
// `net_server_listen` / `net_server_accept` -- the original M1-002 API
// contract never named these two at all (see the updated API CONTRACT
// list below), so there is no stub to swap; they are purely additive.
//
// =============================================================================
// API CONTRACT (per plan §2.1)
// =============================================================================
//
//   net_socket(domain, kind, proto)             -> u64  fd | NET_ERR_*
//   net_bind(sock, sa_va, sa_len)               -> u64  NET_OK | NET_ERR_*
//   net_connect(sock, sa_va, sa_len)            -> u64  NET_OK | NET_ERR_*
//   net_send(sock, buf_va, buf_len)             -> u64  n_sent | NET_ERR_*
//   net_recv(sock, buf_va, buf_len)             -> u64  n_recv | NET_ERR_*
//   net_close(sock)                             -> u64  NET_OK | NET_ERR_*
//   net_inet_pton(str_va, str_len)              -> u64  ipv4_be | NET_ERR_INVAL
//   net_resolve(name_va, name_len, out_va)      -> u64  DNS_OK  | DNS_ERR_*
//   net_tls_wrap(sock, host_va, host_len, trust_cap)
//                                               -> u64  TLS_OK  | TLS_ERR_*
//   net_listen(sock, backlog)                   -> u64  0 | -errno   (M2-002, #5 -- NEW)
//   net_accept(sock)                            -> u64  new_fd | -errno (M2-002, #5 -- NEW)
//
// The four-argument cap on `net_tls_wrap` is deliberately the
// current paideia-as SysV curried-fn ceiling; every other API
// entry sits at three or fewer. A future five-argument variant
// (e.g. to thread an explicit ALPN list) would land as a
// separate symbol carrying packed args in a caller-owned struct
// rather than extending this signature.
//
// =============================================================================
// SWAP DISCIPLINE
// =============================================================================
//
// Each stub carries the target milestone in its justification so
// a grep for the milestone id (`M2-001`, `M3-001`, ...) finds
// every affected stub in one pass on landing. Where the real
// engine can share this file's exact argument registers with no
// translation (M2-001's five SC+ trampolines), the swap replaces
// the `mov rax, <NOT_IMPL>; ret` triplet in-place with a `sub
// rsp,8; call <engine>; add rsp,8; ret` delegation to a
// distinctly-named symbol in its own file (paideia-as has no
// per-module symbol namespacing, so the real engine cannot reuse
// this file's own symbol name once both land in the same
// archive). Where the real engine's natural signature differs from
// this file's original stub contract (M2-004's net_inet_pton,
// whose real engine is out-pointer-shaped rather than
// packed-return-shaped), the swap instead becomes a small adapter
// that preserves this file's original public contract while
// calling through to the differently-shaped engine. Either way,
// callers linking against this file's symbols never re-link.
//
// =============================================================================
// paideia-as compliance
// =============================================================================
//
//   - Module name PascalCase basename (`NetApi`) matches file.
//   - Labels prefixed `libpdxnet_api_` -- disjoint from every other
//     label prefix in this repo, avoids reserved keywords.
//   - No `test rN, rN` -- cmp/je only (encoder pitfall).
//   - Every immediate returned/compared is a small u32 (0x00/0x01/
//     0x0F/0x1F/0x2F); no MOVABS staging needed.
//   - No `fn` with more than 4 curried args -- `net_tls_wrap` is
//     at the ceiling; every other entry sits below.
//   - No byte-register aliases (sil/dil/bl); no `neg`.
//   - String literals are absent from this file; every constant
//     is a bare u64 immediate.
//   - The five M2-001 delegations (net_socket/connect/send/recv/
//     close) and the net_inet_pton adapter each bracket their one
//     nested `call` with `sub rsp,8`/`add rsp,8` (delegations) or a
//     2-push prologue + `sub rsp,24`/`add rsp,24` (the adapter's
//     scratch buffer) -- entry rsp%16==8 in every case, landing
//     rsp%16==0 immediately before the call.
//   - The M2-002 (#5) net_bind adapter uses a 1-push prologue
//     (rbx=sock) with NO further pad -- one push already lands
//     rsp%16==0, the same shape sys_listen.pdx documents. The two NEW
//     net_listen/net_accept delegations use the plain `sub rsp,8`/
//     `add rsp,8` shape (entry rsp%16==8, no push) like every other
//     bare delegation in this file.

module NetApi = structure {

  // === Return-code aliases (from NetTypes.pdx bands) =======================
  //
  // Duplicated as bare u64 constants here so this file assembles
  // cleanly in per-file `paideia-as build --emit elf64` mode
  // without needing cross-module symbol resolution. Values MUST
  // agree with net_types.pdx -- if either constant changes, the
  // other one changes too (grep for the literal 0x0F / 0x1F / 0x2F).
  pub let NETAPI_ERR_NOT_IMPL  : u64 = 0x0F   // == NetTypes.NET_ERR_NOT_IMPL
  pub let NETAPI_DNS_NOT_IMPL  : u64 = 0x1F   // == NetTypes.DNS_ERR_NOT_IMPL
  pub let NETAPI_TLS_NOT_IMPL  : u64 = 0x2F   // == NetTypes.TLS_ERR_NOT_IMPL

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_socket(domain, kind, proto) -> u64
  //
  // Future body: SC+ 87 (sys_socket). AF_INET/SOCK_STREAM at
  // v1; other combinations refuse NET_ERR_INVAL. Returns a
  // fd cap-index on success. Blocked by libpdx-net.M2-001 (#4).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_socket : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (domain: u64, kind: u64, proto: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-001 (#4) SWAP: net_socket now delegates to net_tcp.pdx's net_tcp_socket real SC+87 trampoline, per this file's own documented in-place-swap contract (real body lands at the same stable symbol; callers do not re-link). Landed as a separate net_tcp_* symbol rather than inline here to avoid a duplicate-definition link failure once this file and net_tcp.pdx join the same libpdx-net.a archive -- see net_tcp.pdx's own header for the full rationale. `sub rsp,8; call net_tcp_socket; add rsp,8; ret`: entry rsp%16==8 (no push), pad -> 0 for the call, undo after. Effects widen from `{}` to `{sysreg}` (propagated from net_tcp_socket's own syscall-issuing body). Args (rdi/rsi/rdx) pass through unchanged. No labels needed.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;
        call net_tcp_socket;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_bind(sock, sa_va, sa_len) -> u64
  //
  // SWAP (M2-002, #5): delegates to net_server.pdx's real
  // `net_server_bind(fd, local_port)` engine. The kernel ABI has no
  // sockaddr concept (single configured interface -- see
  // net_server.pdx's own header for the live-code confirmation), so
  // this adapter reads `local_port` as a single 8-byte value pointed
  // to by `sa_va` (the smallest sockaddr-shaped generalization that
  // still matches the real (fd, port) ABI) rather than decoding a
  // POSIX sockaddr_in. `sa_len` is bound-checked >= 8 before the read.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_bind : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{} =
    fn (sock: u64, sa_va: u64, sa_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-002 (#5) SWAP: net_bind adapter over net_server.pdx's real net_server_bind(fd, local_port). Landed as a separate net_server_* symbol rather than inline here to avoid a duplicate-definition link failure once this file and net_server.pdx join the same libpdx-net.a archive -- same rationale net_tcp.pdx's own header documents for M2-001. This stub's ORIGINAL public contract is `(sock, sa_va, sa_len)`; since the live kernel bind ABI is only `(fd, local_port)` with no sockaddr struct at all, the adapter reads local_port as a bare 8-byte value at `[sa_va + 0]` rather than decoding any sockaddr_in-shaped layout -- refuses `sa_len < 8` with NET_ERR_INVAL (0x01) before touching sa_va at all (guards a caller that passed a too-small buffer). 1-push callee-save (rbx=sock): entry rsp%16==8, one push lands rsp%16==0 directly, so the nested call needs no further pad -- the same already-aligned-after-one-push shape sys_listen.pdx's own justification documents. rdi=rbx (sock/fd), rsi=[sa_va+0] (local_port) loaded into rsi before the call. No `test rN,rN` (cmp/jl gate on sa_len). Effects widen from `{}` to `{sysreg, mem}` (sysreg propagated from net_server_bind's own syscall; mem for the one 8-byte read of *sa_va). Labels prefixed libpdxnet_api_bind_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        mov rbx, rdi;                  // sock (fd)

        cmp rdx, 8;
        jl  libpdxnet_api_bind_inval;

        mov rdx, [rsi + 0];            // local_port = *sa_va

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rdx;
        call net_server_bind;

        pop rbx;
        ret;

      libpdxnet_api_bind_inval:
        mov rax, 0x01;                 // NET_ERR_INVAL
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_listen(sock, backlog) -> u64
  //
  // NEW (M2-002, #5): the original M1-002 API contract never named a
  // listen entry point. Plain one-hop delegation to net_server.pdx's
  // net_server_listen, added additively (no stub existed to swap).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_listen : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (sock: u64, backlog: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-002 (#5) NEW: net_listen delegates to net_server.pdx's net_server_listen real SC+89 trampoline. Not part of the original nine-symbol M1-002 API contract -- added additively since a server-side SDK surface needs SOME public listen entry point. `sub rsp,8; call net_server_listen; add rsp,8; ret`: entry rsp%16==8 (no push), pad -> 0 for the call, undo after. Args (rdi/rsi = sock/backlog) pass through unchanged. No labels needed.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;
        call net_server_listen;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_accept(sock) -> u64
  //
  // NEW (M2-002, #5): same additive rationale as net_listen above.
  // Plain one-hop delegation to net_server.pdx's net_server_accept.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_accept : (u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (sock: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-002 (#5) NEW: net_accept delegates to net_server.pdx's net_server_accept real SC+90 trampoline. Not part of the original nine-symbol M1-002 API contract -- added additively (same rationale as net_listen above). `sub rsp,8; call net_server_accept; add rsp,8; ret`: entry rsp%16==8 (no push), pad -> 0 for the call, undo after. Single arg (rdi=sock) passes through unchanged. This syscall blocks kernel-side when no connection is pending (see net_server.pdx's own accept note) -- this delegation does nothing different for that case, it just forwards. No labels needed.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;
        call net_server_accept;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_connect(sock, sa_va, sa_len) -> u64
  //
  // Future body: SC+ 91 (sys_connect). Blocked on M2-001 (#4).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_connect : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (sock: u64, sa_va: u64, sa_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-001 (#4) SWAP: net_connect now delegates to net_tcp.pdx's net_tcp_connect real SC+91 trampoline (same in-place-swap contract and duplicate-symbol rationale as net_socket above). `sub rsp,8; call net_tcp_connect; add rsp,8; ret`; args (rdi/rsi/rdx = sock/sa_va/sa_len, matching net_tcp_connect's fd/remote_ip/remote_port ABI) pass through unchanged. Effects widen from `{}` to `{sysreg}`. No labels needed.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;
        call net_tcp_connect;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_send(sock, buf_va, buf_len) -> u64
  //
  // Future body: SC+ 92 (sys_send). Real body returns bytes
  // actually sent (may be < buf_len for a short write); caller
  // loops on short writes. Blocked on M2-001 (#4).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_send : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (sock: u64, buf_va: u64, buf_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-001 (#4) SWAP: net_send now delegates to net_tcp.pdx's net_tcp_send real SC+92 trampoline (same in-place-swap contract and duplicate-symbol rationale as net_socket above). `sub rsp,8; call net_tcp_send; add rsp,8; ret`; args pass through unchanged. Effects widen from `{}` to `{sysreg}`. No labels needed.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;
        call net_tcp_send;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_recv(sock, buf_va, buf_len) -> u64
  //
  // Future body: SC+ 93 (sys_recv). Real body returns bytes
  // actually received; 0 means peer closed cleanly (mapped to
  // NET_ERR_CLOSED by the wrapper). Blocked on M2-001 (#4).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_recv : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (sock: u64, buf_va: u64, buf_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-001 (#4) SWAP: net_recv now delegates to net_tcp.pdx's net_tcp_recv real SC+93 trampoline (same in-place-swap contract and duplicate-symbol rationale as net_socket above). `sub rsp,8; call net_tcp_recv; add rsp,8; ret`; args pass through unchanged. Effects widen from `{}` to `{sysreg}`. No labels needed.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;
        call net_tcp_recv;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_close(sock) -> u64
  //
  // Future body: SC+ 3 (sys_close, generic fd close). Not
  // milestoned separately; lands with M2-001 (#4).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_close : (u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{} =
    fn (sock: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-001 (#4) SWAP: net_close now delegates to net_tcp.pdx's net_tcp_close real SC+3 trampoline (same in-place-swap contract and duplicate-symbol rationale as net_socket above). `sub rsp,8; call net_tcp_close; add rsp,8; ret`; single arg (rdi=sock) passes through unchanged. Effects widen from `{}` to `{sysreg}`. No labels needed.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;
        call net_tcp_close;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_inet_pton(str_va, str_len) -> u64
  //
  // SWAP (M2-004, #7): dotted-quad IPv4 parse. Real engine landed
  // in net_inet.pdx as a THREE-argument, out-pointer-style
  // `net_inet_pton(str_ptr, str_len, out_be_ptr) -> 0|1` (the
  // canonical signature libpdx-net#7 specifies -- an out-pointer
  // read/write-status shape rather than a packed-return, matching
  // every other multi-output M2 function this same wave lands).
  // That collides on the bare symbol name with THIS file's own
  // two-argument `net_inet_pton` stub, so rather than rename either
  // one, this stub becomes a thin ADAPTER: stack-allocate a 4-byte
  // scratch buffer, call the real net_inet.pdx engine against it,
  // then fold the 4 result bytes back into a single packed-u64
  // return (network byte order: byte0 in bits[31:24] .. byte3 in
  // bits[7:0]) to preserve THIS function's own original two-argument,
  // direct-return public contract (the one downstream tools already
  // link against per the "stable resolution TODAY" promise in this
  // file's header). Returns the packed IPv4 address on success;
  // NET_ERR_INVAL (0x01) on any parse failure. No IPv6 at v1 per
  // plan §2.2.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_inet_pton : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (str_va: u64, str_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M2-004 (#7) SWAP: net_inet_pton adapter over net_inet.pdx's real 3-arg out-pointer engine (named net_inet_pton_raw there specifically to avoid colliding with THIS symbol -- paideia-as has no per-module symbol namespacing, so the two could not both be called net_inet_pton). 2-push callee-save (rbx=str_va, rbp=str_len) then `sub rsp,24` (16-byte scratch buffer for the engine's out_be_ptr + 8 bytes to reach 16-alignment: entry rsp%16==8, two pushes land rsp%16==8, sub 24 -> 0) before `call net_inet_pton_raw` (rdi=rbx, rsi=rbp, rdx=rsp -- the scratch buffer's own address). On rax==0 (success), the 4 bytes at [rsp+0..3] are folded into a single packed-u64 return via four xor-then-mov_b zero-extending loads (#1248 workaround) combined with `shl rax,8; or rax,rcx` per byte, producing byte0<<24|byte1<<16|byte2<<8|byte3 -- the same big-endian-packed-in-a-u64 reading this stub's ORIGINAL (pre-swap) doc comment always promised. On rax!=0 (parse failure), returns NET_ERR_INVAL (0x01) directly without touching the scratch bytes. `add rsp,24; pop rbp; pop rbx; ret` on every path. No `test rN,rN`. Labels prefixed libpdxnet_api_pton_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        mov rbx, rdi;                    // str_va
        mov rbp, rsi;                    // str_len

        sub rsp, 24;                     // scratch[0..3] + alignment pad

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        mov rdx, rsp;                    // out_be_ptr = scratch buffer
        call net_inet_pton_raw;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne libpdxnet_api_pton_fail;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rsp + 0];            // byte0
        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [rsp + 1];            // byte1
        shl rax, 8;
        or  rax, rcx;
        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [rsp + 2];            // byte2
        shl rax, 8;
        or  rax, rcx;
        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [rsp + 3];            // byte3
        shl rax, 8;
        or  rax, rcx;
        jmp libpdxnet_api_pton_done;

      libpdxnet_api_pton_fail:
        mov rax, 1;                      // NET_ERR_INVAL

      libpdxnet_api_pton_done:
        add rsp, 24;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_resolve(name_va, name_len, out_va) -> u64
  //
  // Future body: build DNS query (M2-005 #8 -- LANDED, see
  // net_dns_query.pdx's net_dns_build_query), send over UDP
  // (M2-007 #10 -- still open), parse response (M2-006 #9 --
  // LANDED, see net_dns_parse.pdx's net_dns_parse_response), verify
  // TXID (M3-006 #16 -- already landed). Stores the first A record
  // (4 bytes) at out_va on success. Returns DNS_OK / DNS_ERR_*.
  // Still blocked on M2-007 (#10) plus R100-PREP-002
  // (UDP-via-socket) -- the two pure encode/decode halves this
  // function will compose now both exist, but nothing yet puts a
  // packet on the wire.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_resolve : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (name_va: u64, name_len: u64, out_va: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M1-002 (#2) API stub for net_resolve -- real body blocked on M2-007 (#10) UDP send/recv plus R100-PREP-002 UDP-via-socket. M2-005 (#8) and M2-006 (#9), the query-build and response-parse halves, landed separately in net_dns_query.pdx / net_dns_parse.pdx; this stub is not yet swapped because there is still no UDP transport to drive them over. Returns DNS_ERR_NOT_IMPL (0x1F, small imm32) from the DnsErr band so a caller who folds through the single-u64 return slot sees the failing layer immediately. Leaf. Labels prefixed libpdxnet_api_resolve_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 0x1F;                 // DNS_ERR_NOT_IMPL
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_tls_wrap(sock, host_va, host_len, trust_cap) -> u64
  //
  // Future body: TLS 1.3 handshake over an already-connected
  // socket (ClientHello + ServerHello + key schedule +
  // Ed25519 verify + record-layer seal/open). Emits one
  // TlsHandshakeRecord@0.1 per outcome (M3-005 #15 -- already
  // landed) regardless of verdict. Blocked on M3-001..M3-004
  // (#11/#12/#13/#14) which are themselves blocked on
  // R100-PREP-005 (paideia-as crypto intrinsics).
  //
  // Four-argument entry sits at the paideia-as SysV curried-fn
  // ceiling by design; adding an ALPN list or SNI-override
  // param at v2 will land as a separate symbol carrying packed
  // args in a caller-owned struct.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_tls_wrap : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (sock: u64, host_va: u64, host_len: u64, trust_cap: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M1-002 (#2) API stub for net_tls_wrap -- real body blocked on M3-001..M3-004 (#11/#12/#13/#14) plus R100-PREP-005 (paideia-as crypto intrinsics). Returns TLS_ERR_NOT_IMPL (0x2F, small imm32) from the TlsErr band so a caller who folds through the single-u64 return slot sees the failing layer immediately. Four args at the paideia-as SysV curried-fn ceiling by design; a v2 five-arg variant lands as a separate symbol with packed args. Leaf. Labels prefixed libpdxnet_api_tls_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 0x2F;                 // TLS_ERR_NOT_IMPL
        ret
      }
    }

}
